BE-300 2.4.18: replace inline-asm ip_fast_csum with portable C.

The original include/asm-mips/checksum.h's ip_fast_csum() is hand-written
inline asm with output operands `"=&r" (sum), "=&r" (iph), "=&r" (ihl),
"=&r" (dummy)` and matching inputs `"1" (iph), "2" (ihl)`.  Under modern
GCC (verified with gcc 10.3.0 from the Debian mipsel-linux-gnu toolchain
plus -fgnu89-inline + -O2) the asm produces a sum byte pattern that does
not match what the receiver's inlined ip_fast_csum re-computes for the
same header.  The kernel boots fine and listen() succeeds, but every
loopback connect() times out because ip_rcv silently drops the SYN
packets with InHdrErrors (csum != 0).

  Concrete observation on a `socket+bind+listen / fork / connect`
  self-test: child's connect() returns ETIMEDOUT (-145), and after the
  test /proc/net/snmp shows
      Ip: ... InReceives=3 InHdrErrors=3 ... InDelivers=0
  The same userspace runs fine on the original linux4be vmlinux (built
  with gcc 3.0.1 in 2002) and on real BE-300 silicon, so the kernel
  source itself is good — only the gcc-10 codegen for this particular
  asm constraint pattern is bad.

Replacing the asm with a small portable C version produces the
arithmetically-equivalent checksum and is fast enough on the VR4131 to
have no measurable boot-time impact.  TCP loopback then works
end-to-end: a bare TCP self-test reports `connect OK`, /proc/net/snmp
shows InDelivers > 0, and the picogui-demo userspace (booted from JFFS2
on /dev/mtdblock3) now reaches its full launcher with pgboard / omnibar /
canvastst all connected.

Bisection that led here:
  * 0003, 0005, 0008, 0009, 0010 individually-skipped: no change (still
    white screen / connect ETIMEDOUT).
  * 0006 skipped: JFFS2 mount fails (required for any boot).
  * Rebuild with -O1 / -O0 / -Os: no change or build failure.
  * Reverting the patch-0001 `extern __inline__ → static __inline__`
    conversion in asm-mips/{bitops,atomic,system,pgalloc}.h: no change.
  * Adding ip_rcv InHdrError-path printk diagnostics: revealed that
    every loopback packet failed ip_fast_csum() validation.
  * Computed expected vs stored checksum: the diff was the value of one
    of the IP-header bytes (e.g. 0x40 = DF-flag byte), suggesting the
    asm path was either missing or double-counting that byte under
    modern gcc register allocation.
